  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 956 PM MST Wed Nov 4 2026\n\nTemperatures will drop through the rest of the night as the\nshortwave trough moves past New Mexico. Some lowland desert spots\nin the Tularosa and Jornada could fall into the upper 30s, though\nno new freeze areas are expected. High temperatures for Thursday \nwill run about 2 to 5 degrees below average (upper 60s-low 70s), \nwith the exception of the Lower Valley, which will run about 1 to \n2 degrees above seasonal averages (mid to upper 70s). Winds will \nbe generally from the north at 5 to 10 mph, and conditions will be\nrelatively dry.\n\nTemperatures will fall again on Friday, close to 4 to 6 degrees\nbelow average. A deep trough and weak southeasterly push or backdoor \ncold front will bring increasing chances for light rain showers \nFriday night into Saturday. The GFS has caught up with the ECMWF \nwith cutting off the trough into a cutoff low and slowing it down \nover the region Friday night and into Saturday, increasing the QPF\nto around 0.1 for the lowlands. The next couple of runs should \nsee the QPF increase, so we will need to keep watch for how heavy \nthis rain event will be. PWATS at this time for Friday and \nSaturday are hovering around 0.6\", so flooding is unlikely to be a\nrisk, though it can't be ruled out if the QPF gets heavier. Dry \nweather returns on Sunday as an upper-level high moves into the \nregion.",
